<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2127"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 17, 2004 - 3:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The Robin Williams movie is &#34;One Hour Photo&#34;.  I think it likely that BTK took the photo himself during his surveillance, using a telephoto lens.  I do a fair amount of wildlife and astronomy photography and with practice it is possible to get very clear photos.  I am convinced BTK had to do quite a bit of surveillance on each of the homes and neighborhoods of the victims.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
